 "genre_note": "76 notes over 11 documents; 92.7% of cluster words assigned (14,345/15,483), verified programmatically. Per-doc: doc_0220 99.2%, doc_0475 99.1%, doc_0478 98.3%, doc_0277 96.5%, doc_0260 96.2%, doc_0307 93.9%, doc_0443 90.0%, doc_0538 89.5%, doc_0445 86.7%, doc_0352 83.7%, doc_0362 76.1%. Four documents land below 90% for structural reasons, not careless deletion: doc_0362 (Sportskeeda) carries 234 words of betting promos, a draft-simulator ad, a start/sit plug and a 167-word prediction-game terms-and-conditions block; doc_0352 (Guardian) carries a 90-word newsletter promotion plus an 84-word \"how to turn on sport notifications\" app guide; doc_0445 and doc_0538 (both Independent) each open with roughly 85 words of newsletter sign-up boilerplate and Mustache template error strings. Everything else dropped is article titles (carried in the note H1), bylines, \"Also Read\" / \"Watch This Story\" / \"Embed from Getty Images\" furniture, ADVERTISEMENT separators, contentless section headers, decorative team tweets, and one empty timestamp (doc_0307 block 213).\n\nBuilding-block distribution matches the genre. 64 of 76 notes are empirical_observation: dated events, scorelines, transfer fees, auction hammer prices, quotes, injury lists. 5 are concept (the Mr. Irrelevant draft title, High Limit Racing as an entity, the Premier League's Indian broadcast and streaming arrangement, the IPL 2024 auction's venue/date/slot structure, and Ireland's Rugby World Cup quarter-final curse as a named phenomenon). 5 are argument (the Sportskeeda draft-class grades, which are claims with grounds rather than facts; Jedd Fisch on what beating Utah signifies about his program; the Independent's \"greatest Ireland team, yet no knockout win\" thesis; the IPL pundit previews, each reasoning from a stated ground such as \"RCB will focus on the bowlers because it is difficult to defend at the Chinnaswamy Stadium\"; and the claim that English and Australian players' mid-tournament unavailability should depress their bids). 1 is counter_argument (Jamie Carragher calling Ronaldo's Saudi move a \"sad end\" \u2014 the only genuine rebuttal in the cluster). 1 is model (the causal chain by which High Limit Racing's $50,000 purses pressure weekend sprint-car promoters to raise their own purses, lift team merchandise revenue, and keep small dirt tracks maintained).\n\nTwo blocks were NOT manufactured: there is no procedure and no hypothesis anywhere in this cluster. The closest candidate to procedure was doc_0260's \"subscribe to Hotstar VIP to stream\" passage, which describes rights and channels rather than ordered steps, so it is filed as concept. The closest to hypothesis was doc_0443's closing speculation about Larson's post-NASCAR hopes for the series \u2014 an aspiration, not a testable prediction \u2014 so it sits in the High Limit Racing concept note. Absence here is a finding, not a gap.\n\nThree roundup/live-blog documents drove the decomposition and were split on topical, not size, grounds. doc_0277 is a seven-item ranked listicle: each ranked player is a separate subject, so it became eight player notes plus a race-framing note, never one \"top scorers\" note. doc_0352 is a four-league European roundup \u2014 Bundesliga, LaLiga, Serie A and Ligue 1 items share nothing but the page \u2014 so it became four match notes plus two cross-document player notes. doc_0307 is a 255-block, 3,696-word IPL 2024 auction live blog: it was cut along the auction's own structure (capped batters, all-rounders, wicketkeepers, pace and spin, the uncapped phase, two accelerated rounds), with the two record signings and the purse tracker lifted out; because that yields 18 notes it is split across two sub-plans as required.\n\nThree notes are shared entity notes drawing blocks from two documents each, per Step 3: harry_kane_bayern_munich_2023_scoring_records (doc_0277 blocks 39-48 plus doc_0352 block 6, his eighth Bundesliga goal and the Haaland comparison); lautaro_martinez_2023_goalscoring_form (doc_0277 blocks 9-13 plus doc_0352 blocks 13-20 \u2014 the whole Inter item in doc_0352 is in substance a Lautaro item, so no separate Salernitana match note was created); cole_palmer_penalty_taking_role_and_record (doc_0445 blocks 8-9 plus doc_0478 block 19, the deflected 35th-minute penalty at Tottenham). No (doc, block) pair is assigned twice, and assigned-plus-dropped exactly partitions all 553 blocks \u2014 both checked by script.\n\nOne block deserves flagging for whoever writes the notes. doc_0478 is headlined \"Tottenham's Unbeaten Run Ends With 4-1 Home Loss to Chelsea\", but its own summary block 1 says Tottenham \"suffered their first loss of the Premier League campaign in a fiery 2-1 defeat\". The body unambiguously supports 4-1 (Kulusevski, Palmer's penalty, then Jackson at 75', 94' and 97'). The 2-1 is a publisher error rather than a scope condition, but block 1 is retained and assigned rather than silently deleted, and it carries two load-bearing facts the headline omits: Spurs finished with nine men, and this ended their unbeaten start. The note should record 4-1 and may note the source's internal inconsistency.",
